<section xml:id="sec-logical-expressions">
	<title>Logical Expressions</title>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-logical-values">
		<title>Logical Values</title>
		<p>
			Before MATLAB can make a decision, it needs a way to represent the answer to a yes-or-no question. Those answers are called <term>logical values</term>: <pf>true</pf> and <pf>false</pf>. They are the foundation of every conditional statement you will write.
		</p>

		<p>
			So far, we've written code that runs step by step in a straight line. Real programs
			need to react to data or user input, choosing different actions when conditions change.
		</p>

		<p>
			MATLAB has two logical values:
			<pf>true</pf> and <pf>false</pf>. When displayed, they often appear as <pf>logical 1</pf> and <pf>logical 0</pf>.
			Although they behave similarly to the numeric values <pf>1</pf> and <pf>0</pf>, they are different data types.
			Just as numbers can be stored in variables, logical values can be stored in variables as well.
		</p>

		<assemblage xml:id="logical-values-structure">
			<title>Logical Values in MATLAB</title>

			<p>
				MATLAB has two <term>logical values</term>: <pf>true</pf> and <pf>false</pf>. When displayed, they often appear as <pf>logical 1</pf> and <pf>logical 0</pf>.
				Although they behave similarly to the numeric values <pf>1</pf> and <pf>0</pf>, they have a different data type: <pf>logical</pf> instead of <pf>double</pf>.
			</p>

			<program language="matlab">
				a = true;    % logical value: true
				b = false;   % logical value: false
			</program>

			<p>
				Key rules:
				<ul>
					<li>Logical values are of type <pf>logical</pf>, not <pf>double</pf>.</li>
					<li><pf>true</pf> and <pf>1</pf> display the same but are different data types.</li>
					<li><pf>false</pf> and <pf>0</pf> display the same but are different data types.</li>
					<li>Use <pf>whos</pf> to inspect variable class and memory usage.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>

		</assemblage>

		<p>
			Just as numbers can be stored in variables, logical values can be stored in variables as well. The code below stores both numeric and logical values so you can compare them:
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
			a = 1;
			b = true;
			c = 0;
			d = false;

			fprintf("a = %g,  b = %g,  c = %g,  d = %g\n", a, b, c, d)
		</program>

		<p>
			Running the above code, <pf>fprintf</pf> produces the following output:
		</p>

		<pre>
			a = 1,  b = 1,  c = 0,  d = 0
		</pre>

		<p>
			Even though <pf>a</pf> and <pf>b</pf> (and <pf>c</pf> and <pf>d</pf>) look the same, MATLAB treats them differently.
			You can get more information using the <pf>whos</pf> command. Here is the output you should see:
		</p>

		<pre>
			Name        Size            Bytes  Class      Attributes
			a           1x1                 8  double
			b           1x1                 1  logical
			c           1x1                 8  double
			d           1x1                 1  logical
		</pre>

		<p>
			This shows us two differences: 
			<dl width="wide">
				<li><title>Class (data type)</title>
					<p><pf>a</pf> is a <pf>double</pf>, while <pf>b</pf> is a <pf>logical</pf>.</p>
				</li>
				<li><title>Bytes (memory usage)</title>
					<p><pf>a</pf> uses 8 bytes, while <pf>b</pf> uses 1 byte.</p>
				</li>
			</dl>
			The <pf>double</pf> data type is meant to store numeric quantities, while the <pf>logical</pf> data type stores yes/no answers to questions.
		</p>

		<exercises xml:id="conceptual-questions-logical-values">
			<title>🤔💭 Conceptual Questions</title>

			<exercise label="concepts-logical-values">
				<task label="logical-values-reading-ckpt-1a">
					<statement correct="no">
						<p>
							In MATLAB, <pf>true</pf> and <pf>1</pf> are the same data type.
						</p>
					</statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>
							False. While <pf>true</pf> displays as <pf>1</pf>, it is of type <pf>logical</pf>, not <pf>double</pf>. They have different data types even though they display the same way.
						</p>
					</feedback>
				</task>

				<task label="logical-values-reading-ckpt-1b">
					<statement>
						<p>
							Which of the following correctly describes the difference between <pf>true</pf> (logical) and <pf>1</pf> (double)?
						</p>
					</statement>
					<choices randomize="yes">
						<choice correct="yes">
							<statement>
								<p><pf>true</pf> represents a yes/no answer; <pf>1</pf> represents a numeric quantity.</p>
							</statement>
							<feedback>
								<p>Correct! Logical values are designed for yes/no answers, while numeric values represent measurements or counts.</p>
							</feedback>
						</choice>
						<choice>
							<statement>
								<p>They are identical and can be used interchangeably in all contexts.</p>
							</statement>
							<feedback>
								<p>Incorrect. While they often behave similarly, they are different data types with different purposes.</p>
							</feedback>
						</choice>
						<choice>
							<statement>
								<p><pf>true</pf> uses more memory than <pf>1</pf>.</p>
							</statement>
							<feedback>
								<p>Incorrect. A <pf>logical</pf> value uses 1 byte; a <pf>double</pf> uses 8 bytes. Logical values use <em>less</em> memory.</p>
							</feedback>
						</choice>
						<choice>
							<statement>
								<p>MATLAB displays <pf>true</pf> as <pf>true</pf> and <pf>1</pf> as <pf>1</pf>.</p>
							</statement>
							<feedback>
								<p>Incorrect. Both <pf>true</pf> and <pf>1</pf> are displayed as <pf>1</pf> when used with <pf>fprintf</pf>.</p>
							</feedback>
						</choice>
					</choices>
				</task>

				<task label="logical-values-reading-ckpt-1c">
					<statement>
						<p>
							Which MATLAB command displays the class (data type) and memory size of all variables in the workspace?
						</p>
					</statement>
					<choices randomize="yes">
						<choice correct="yes">
							<statement><p><pf>whos</pf></p></statement>
							<feedback><p>Correct! <pf>whos</pf> lists every variable with its size, byte count, and class.</p></feedback>
						</choice>
						<choice>
							<statement><p><pf>disp</pf></p></statement>
							<feedback><p><pf>disp</pf> prints the value of a variable but does not show its type or memory usage.</p></feedback>
						</choice>
						<choice>
							<statement><p><pf>class</pf></p></statement>
							<feedback><p><pf>class</pf> returns the data type of a single variable but does not list the whole workspace.</p></feedback>
						</choice>
						<choice>
							<statement><p><pf>size</pf></p></statement>
							<feedback><p><pf>size</pf> returns the dimensions of an array, not the data type.</p></feedback>
						</choice>
					</choices>
				</task>
			</exercise>

		</exercises>

		<example xml:id="ex-logical-values-store">
			<title>Storing and Displaying Logical Values</title>

			<statement>
				<p>
					The following example shows how logical values are stored and how they differ from numeric values when inspected with <pf>whos</pf>:
				</p>

				<program language="matlab">
					isRaining = true;
					temperature = 22;

					whos isRaining temperature
				</program>

				<p>
					Expected output:
				</p>

				<pre>
Name            Size    Bytes  Class      Attributes
isRaining       1x1         1  logical
temperature     1x1         8  double
				</pre>

				<p>
					Notice that <pf>isRaining</pf> is of type <pf>logical</pf> and uses only 1 byte, while <pf>temperature</pf> is of type <pf>double</pf> and uses 8 bytes.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</example>

		<example xml:id="ex-logical-values-arithmetic">
			<title>Logical Values in Arithmetic</title>

			<statement>
				<p>
					MATLAB allows logical values to participate in arithmetic expressions, treating <pf>true</pf> as <pf>1</pf> and <pf>false</pf> as <pf>0</pf>. This can be useful for counting:
				</p>

				<program language="matlab">
					a = true;
					b = false;

					fprintf("a + b = %g\n", a + b)   % 1 + 0 = 1
					fprintf("a * 5 = %g\n", a * 5)   % 1 * 5 = 5
					fprintf("b * 5 = %g\n", b * 5)   % 0 * 5 = 0
				</program>

				<p>
					While this works, keep in mind that the result of arithmetic on logical values is of type <pf>double</pf>, not <pf>logical</pf>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</example>

		<exercise label="logical-values-practice-1">
			<title>Logical Value Identification</title>

			<task label="logical-values-practice-1a">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Without running the code, predict the output of the following:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						x = false;
						fprintf("x = %g\n", x)
					</program>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<p>
						Output: <pf>x = 0</pf>. The logical value <pf>false</pf> displays as <pf>0</pf> when printed with <pf>%g</pf>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

		</exercise>
		<exercise label="logical-values-activity-1">
			<title>Recognizing Logical vs. Numeric Types</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					Use the coding area below to explore how MATLAB distinguishes logical values from numeric ones.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task label="logical-values-activity-1a">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Define variables <pf>p = true</pf>, <pf>q = 1</pf>, <pf>r = false</pf>, and <pf>s = 0</pf>. Then run <pf>whos</pf> to inspect their types.
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							% ==== Type your code here ====

							% =============================
						</input>
					</sage>
					<p>
						Confirm that <pf>p</pf> and <pf>r</pf> have class <pf>logical</pf>, while <pf>q</pf> and <pf>s</pf> have class <pf>double</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						p = true;
						q = 1;
						r = false;
						s = 0;
						whos p q r s
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="logical-values-activity-1b">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Add the lines <pf>fprintf("p = %g\n", p)</pf> and <pf>fprintf("q = %g\n", q)</pf>. Confirm both print <pf>1</pf> even though they have different types.
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							% ==== Type your code here ====

							% =============================
						</input>
					</sage>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						p = true;
						q = 1;
						fprintf("p = %g\n", p)
						fprintf("q = %g\n", q)
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

		</exercise>
	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-relational-operators">
		<title>Relational Operators</title>

		<p>
			A <term>logical expression</term> is any MATLAB expression that produces a <pf>logical</pf> value, 
			either <pf>true</pf> or <pf>false</pf>.
			The most common logical expressions come from comparing two values. These comparisons use
			<term>relational operators</term>.
		</p>

		<assemblage xml:id="logical-statements-structure">
			<title>Logical Statements: Relational and Logical Operators</title>

			<p>
				The most common logical statements come from comparing two values using <term>relational operators</term>:
			</p>

			<table xml:id="tab-relational-operators0">
				<title>Relational operators in MATLAB</title>

				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
					<col width="25%" halign="center"/>
					<col width="25%" halign="center"/>
					<col width="50%" halign="left"/>
					<row header="yes">
						<cell>Operator</cell>
						<cell>Example</cell>
						<cell>Question being asked</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>==</pf></cell>
						<cell><pf>x == y</pf></cell>
						<cell>Is <m>\texttt{x}</m> equal to <m>\texttt{y}</m>?</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>~=</pf></cell>
						<cell><pf>x ~= y</pf></cell>
						<cell>Is <m>\texttt{x}</m> not equal to <m>\texttt{y}</m>?</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>&lt;</pf></cell>
						<cell><pf>x &lt; y</pf></cell>
						<cell>Is <m>\texttt{x}</m> less than <m>\texttt{y}</m>?</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>&lt;=</pf></cell>
						<cell><pf>x &lt;= y</pf></cell>
						<cell>Is <m>\texttt{x}</m> less than or equal to <m>\texttt{y}</m>?</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>&gt;</pf></cell>
						<cell><pf>x &gt; y</pf></cell>
						<cell>Is <m>\texttt{x}</m> greater than <m>\texttt{y}</m>?</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>&gt;=</pf></cell>
						<cell><pf>x &gt;= y</pf></cell>
						<cell>Is <m>\texttt{x}</m> greater than or equal to <m>\texttt{y}</m>?</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</table>

			<p>
				Logical statements can be combined using <term>logical operators</term>:
			</p>

			<table xml:id="tab-logical-operators0">
				<title>Logical operators for combining statements</title>

				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
					<col width="15%" halign="center"/>
					<col width="40%" halign="center"/>
					<col width="45%" halign="left"/>
					<row header="yes">
						<cell>Operator</cell>
						<cell>Example</cell>
						<cell>Question being asked</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>~</pf></cell>
						<cell><pf>~ans1</pf></cell>
						<cell>Is <m>\texttt{ans1}</m> NOT true?</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>&amp;</pf></cell>
						<cell><pf>ans1 &amp; ans2</pf></cell>
						<cell>Are BOTH <m>\texttt{ans1}</m> and <m>\texttt{ans2}</m> true?</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>|</pf></cell>
						<cell><pf>ans1 | ans2</pf></cell>
						<cell>Is AT LEAST ONE of <m>\texttt{ans1}</m> or <m>\texttt{ans2}</m> true?</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</table>

			<p>
				Key rules:
				<ul>
					<li>Use <pf>=</pf> (single) to assign a value; use <pf>==</pf> (double) to compare values.</li>
					<li>NOT (<pf>~</pf>) is evaluated first, then AND (<pf>&amp;</pf>), then OR (<pf>|</pf>). Use parentheses to override this order.</li>
					<li>Every logical expression produces a <pf>logical</pf> value: <pf>true</pf> or <pf>false</pf>.</li>
					<li>Use parentheses around complex expressions to improve readability.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>

		</assemblage>

		<exercise label="logical-statements-reading-ckpt-1">

			<task label="logical-statements-reading-ckpt-1a">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Which of the following statements is correct about <pf>=</pf> and <pf>==</pf>?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement>
							<p><pf>x = 5</pf> assigns 5 to <pf>x</pf>, while <pf>x == 5</pf> tests whether <pf>x</pf> equals 5.</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>Correct! Single <pf>=</pf> is for assignment; double <pf>==</pf> is for comparison.</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p><pf>x = 5</pf> and <pf>x == 5</pf> do the same thing.</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>Incorrect. These operators have completely different purposes.</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p><pf>x == 5</pf> assigns 5 to <pf>x</pf> only if <pf>x</pf> doesn't already have a value.</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>Incorrect. The <pf>==</pf> operator never assigns values; it only compares them.</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p><pf>x == 5</pf> sets <pf>x</pf> to 5 and checks if the assignment succeeded.</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>Incorrect. <pf>==</pf> is purely a comparison operator and never assigns values.</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="logical-statements-reading-ckpt-1b">
				<statement>
					<p>
						According to MATLAB's operator precedence, in what order are the logical operators <pf>~</pf>, <pf>&amp;</pf>, and <pf>|</pf> evaluated?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p>NOT first, then AND, then OR.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Correct! The precedence order is <pf>~</pf>, then <pf>&amp;</pf>, then <pf>|</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>OR first, then AND, then NOT.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Incorrect. NOT has the highest precedence, not OR.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>AND first, then NOT, then OR.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Incorrect. NOT is evaluated before AND.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>All operators have equal precedence.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Incorrect. They have different precedence: NOT, then AND, then OR.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="logical-statements-reading-ckpt-1c">
				<statement correct="yes">
					<p>
						The expression <pf>~(x &gt; 10)</pf> is equivalent to <pf>x &lt;= 10</pf>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						True. Negating "greater than" gives "less than or equal to."
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

		</exercise>

		<p>
			Here are a few comparison examples. The parentheses are optional, but they help clarify which parts of the expression are being compared.
		</p>

		<example xml:id="ex-relational-operators">
			<title>Using Relational Operators</title>

			<statement>
				<p>
					The following code shows how each relational operator produces a logical result:
				</p>

				<program language="matlab">
					a = 4;
					b = -4;

					ans1 = (a == 4);    % Is a equal to 4?    Yes → true  (1)
					ans2 = (a == b);    % Is a equal to b?    No  → false (0)
					ans3 = (b &lt; a);    % Is b less than a?  Yes → true  (1)
					ans4 = (a ~= b);    % Is a not equal to b? Yes → true (1)
					ans5 = (a &gt;= 5);  % Is a &gt;= 5?      No  → false (0)
				</program>

			</statement>
		</example>

		<example xml:id="ex-combining-logical-statements">
			<title>Combining Logical Statements</title>

			<statement>
				<p>
					Use <pf>&amp;</pf> (AND) and <pf>|</pf> (OR) to ask multi-part questions:
				</p>

				<program language="matlab">
					P = 1;
					Q = 10;
					R = -4;

					% AND examples
					P &lt;= R &amp; P &gt; Q    % false &amp; false → 0
					R &lt; 0 &amp; R &lt; Q    % true &amp; true  → 1

					% OR examples
					Q == R | Q == P    % false | false → 0
					P &lt; Q | P &lt; R    % true  | false → 1

					% NOT example
					~(P &lt; Q &amp; P &lt; R)  % NOT (true &amp; false) → 1
				</program>

				<p>
					The truth table for all combinations:
				</p>

				<table>
					<title>Logical Operators Truth Table</title>
					<tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
						<col halign="center"/>
						<col halign="center"/>
						<col halign="center"/>
						<col halign="center"/>
						<col halign="center"/>
						<row header="yes">
							<cell colspan="2">Logical Variables</cell>
							<cell colspan="3">Operator Results</cell>
						</row>
						<row header="yes">
							<cell><pf>ans1</pf></cell>
							<cell><pf>ans2</pf></cell>
							<cell><pf>ans1 &amp; ans2</pf></cell>
							<cell><pf>ans1 | ans2</pf></cell>
							<cell><pf>~ans1</pf></cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><pf>1</pf></cell><cell><pf>1</pf></cell><cell><pf>1</pf></cell><cell><pf>1</pf></cell><cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><pf>1</pf></cell><cell><pf>0</pf></cell><cell><pf>0</pf></cell><cell><pf>1</pf></cell><cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><pf>0</pf></cell><cell><pf>1</pf></cell><cell><pf>0</pf></cell><cell><pf>1</pf></cell><cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><pf>0</pf></cell><cell><pf>0</pf></cell><cell><pf>0</pf></cell><cell><pf>0</pf></cell><cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
				</table>
			</statement>
		</example>

		<exercise label="logical-statements-practice-1">
			<title>Evaluate Logical Expressions</title>

			<task label="logical-statements-practice-1a">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Given <pf>x = 5</pf> and <pf>y = 3</pf>, what does <pf>(x &gt; y) &amp; (y ~= 0)</pf> evaluate to?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<p>
						<pf>1</pf> (true). Both parts are true: <pf>5 &gt; 3</pf> is true and <pf>3 ~= 0</pf> is true.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="logical-statements-practice-1b">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Given <pf>a = 7</pf>, what does <pf>(a &lt; 5) | (a &gt; 10)</pf> evaluate to?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<p>
						<pf>0</pf> (false). Neither part is true: <pf>7 &lt; 5</pf> is false and <pf>7 &gt; 10</pf> is false.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</exercise>

		<table xml:id="tab-relational-operators">
			<title>Relational operators in MATLAB</title>

			<tabular>
				<col width="25%" halign="center"/>
				<col width="25%" halign="center"/>
				<col width="50%" halign="left"/>
				<row header="yes">
					<cell>Relational operator</cell>
					<cell>Logical expression</cell>
					<cell>Question being asked</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>==</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>x == y</pf></cell>
					<cell>Is <m>\texttt{x}</m> equal to <m>\texttt{y}</m>?</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>~=</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>x ~= y</pf></cell>
					<cell>Is <m>\texttt{x}</m> not equal to <m>\texttt{y}</m>?</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>&lt;</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>x &lt; y</pf></cell>
					<cell>Is <m>\texttt{x}</m> less than <m>\texttt{y}</m>?</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>&lt;=</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>x &lt;= y</pf></cell>
					<cell>Is <m>\texttt{x}</m> less than or equal to <m>\texttt{y}</m>?</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>&gt;</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>x &gt; y</pf></cell>
					<cell>Is <m>\texttt{x}</m> greater than <m>\texttt{y}</m>?</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>&gt;=</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>x &gt;= y</pf></cell>
					<cell><p><m>\text{Is}\ \texttt{x}\ \text{greater than or equal to}\ \texttt{y} \text{?}</m></p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
		</table>

		<p>
			A common beginner mistake is confusing the assignment operator <pf>=</pf> with the 
			comparison operator <pf>==</pf>. The single equal sign assigns a value to a variable 
			(telling MATLAB what to store), while the double equal sign compares two values 
			(asking MATLAB a question).
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
			cost = 7.1;     % Telling: assign a value
			cost == 7.1     % Asking: compare a value
		</program>

		<p>
			Here are a few comparison examples. The parentheses are optional, but they help clarify
			which parts of the expression are being compared.
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
			a = 4; 
			b = -4;

			ans1 = (a == 4);	% ← Is a equal to 4?	Yes.
			ans2 = (a == b);	% ← Is a equal to b?	No.
			ans3 = (b &lt; a);		% ← Is b less than a?	Yes.
		</program>

		<p>
			This code defines two <pf>double</pf> variables, <pf>a</pf> and <pf>b</pf>, and three <pf>logical</pf> variables:

			<ul marker="square">
				<li><pf>ans1 = 1</pf> since the logical expression <pf>a == 4</pf> is true.</li>
				<li><pf>ans2 = 0</pf> since the logical expression <pf>a == b</pf> is false.</li>
				<li><pf>ans3 = 1</pf> since the logical expression <pf>b &lt; a</pf> is true.</li>
			</ul>
		</p>

		<exercise label="ckpt-logical-expressions-2">
			<title>Check Your Understanding: Logical Expressions</title>

			<task label="logic-ckpt-intro">
				<title>Logical Expressions</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Let <pf>x</pf>, <pf>y</pf>, and <pf>z</pf> be numeric variables. Which of the following are valid logical expressions in MATLAB?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>x = y</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. The expression <pf>x = y</pf> uses a single equal sign, which is for assignment, not comparison.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>z &gt; y</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! The expression <pf>z &gt; y</pf> is a valid logical expression that asks if <pf>z</pf> is greater than <pf>y</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>y =&lt; 2</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. The expression <pf>y =&lt; 2</pf> uses an incorrect operator; the correct operator for "less than or equal to" is <pf>&lt;=</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>z ~= z</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! The expression <pf>z ~= z</pf> is a valid logical expression that asks if <pf>z</pf> is not equal to <pf>z</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>x ~ z</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. The expression <pf>x ~ z</pf> is not a valid logical expression because it lacks a proper relational operator.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What is the result of the following logical expression in MATLAB?
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						p = 10 &gt; 5;
					</program>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>1</pf> (logical)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! Since 10 is indeed greater than 5, the expression evaluates to <pf>true</pf> (logical 1).
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>0</pf> (logical)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. Remember that 10 is greater than 5, so the expression evaluates to <pf>true</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>1</pf> (double)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. While <pf>1</pf> (double) and <pf>true</pf> (logical 1) may seem similar, they are different data types in MATLAB.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>0</pf> (double)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. The expression evaluates to <pf>true</pf> (logical 1), not <pf>false</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What is the result of the following logical expression in MATLAB?
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						r = (pi ~= 3.14);
					</program>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>1</pf> (logical)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! Since 3.14 is only an approximation of pi, it is not equal to pi.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>0</pf> (logical)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. Since 3.14 is only an approximation of pi, it is not exactly equal to the 
								value of pi in MATLAB, so the expression evaluates to true.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>1</pf> (double)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. While <pf>1</pf> (double) and <pf>true</pf> (logical 1) may seem similar, they are different data types in MATLAB.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>0</pf> (double)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. The expression evaluates to true (logical 1), not false, since pi and 3.14 
								are not exactly equal.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>
		</exercise>
	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-negating-expressions">
		<title>Negating Logical Expressions</title>

		<p>
			To ask the opposite of a logical expression, use the NOT operator <pf>~</pf>.
			If an expression is true, its negation is false, and vice versa.
		</p>

		<program language="matlab">
			~true	% returns false
			~false	% returns true
		</program>

		<p>
			The NOT operator negates a single logical value or statement. When the statement is a comparison, 
			use parentheses so MATLAB negates the whole comparison.
		</p>

		<p>
			For example, the following expressions ask if <q>8 is not equal to 9</q>:
			<ul marker="square">
				<li><pf>~(8 == 9)</pf> <m>\quad\Rightarrow\quad</m> <q>NOT (8 is equal to 9)</q></li>
				<li><pf>8 ~= 9</pf> <m>\quad\Rightarrow\quad</m> <q>8 is not equal to 9</q></li>
			</ul>
		</p>

		<p>
			Similarly, the negation of <pf>x &gt;= 5</pf> is <pf>x &lt; 5</pf>, so the two expressions are equivalent:
			<ul marker="square">
				<li><pf>~(x &gt;= 5)</pf> <m>\quad\Rightarrow\quad</m> <q>NOT (x is greater than or equal to 5)</q></li>
				<li><pf>x &lt; 5</pf> <m>\quad\Rightarrow\quad</m> <q>x is less than 5</q></li>
			</ul>
		</p>

		<p>
			When negating expressions, use parentheses to make your intent clear and improve the readability of your code.
		</p>

		<exercise label="ckpt-negating-expressions-1">
			<title>Negating Logical Expressions</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Which of the following expressions correctly asks "Is <pf>x</pf> NOT greater than 10?"
					Select all that apply.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement>
						<p><pf>~(x &gt; 10)</pf></p>
					</statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>Correct! This uses the NOT operator to negate the comparison.</p>
					</feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement>
						<p><pf>x &lt;= 10</pf></p>
					</statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>Correct! This is the equivalent relational operator form without negation.</p>
					</feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement>
						<p><pf>~x &gt; 10</pf></p>
					</statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>Incorrect. Without parentheses, this negates x first, then compares the result to 10, 
						which is not the intended meaning.</p>
					</feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement>
						<p><pf>x &lt; 10</pf></p>
					</statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>Incorrect. This asks if x is strictly less than 10, which excludes the case where x equals 10.</p>
					</feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>

	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-combining-two-expressions">
		<title>Combining Logical Expressions</title>

			<p>
				Many questions involve multiple conditions. For example, to determine if a number is an even number greater than 100,
				then you need to check two things at once:
				<ul marker="square">
					<li>Is the number even?</li>
					<li>Is the number greater than 100?</li>
				</ul>
				Because both must be true, we join these expressions with <q>and</q>. 
				If either of these conditions could be true, then we would join them with <q>or</q>. 
			</p>

			<p>
				MATLAB joins logical expressions with AND and OR using the operators <pf>&amp;</pf> (AND) and <pf>|</pf> (OR). 
			</p>

			<table xml:id="tab-logical-operators">
				<title>Logical operators for combining expressions</title>

				<tabular>
					<col width="15%" halign="center"/>
					<col width="40%" halign="center"/>
					<col width="45%" halign="left"/>
					<row>
						<cell><p><m>\textbf{Logical}</m> <m>\textbf{operator}</m></p></cell>
						<cell><p><m>\textbf{Logical expression}</m></p></cell>
						<cell><p><m>\textbf{Question being asked}</m></p></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>&amp;</pf></cell>
						<cell><p><pf>ans1 &amp; ans2</pf></p></cell>
						<cell>Are both <m>\texttt{ans1}</m> and <m>\texttt{ans2}</m> true?</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell><pf>|</pf></cell>
						<cell><pf>ans1 | ans2</pf></cell>
						<cell><m>\text{Is at least one of}\ \texttt{ans1}\ \text{or}\ \texttt{ans2}\ \text{true?}</m></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</table>

			<p>
				The result of combining logical expressions are exactly what you would expect from everyday language:
				AND is true only when both sides are true, while OR is true when at least one side is true.
			</p>

			<program language="matlab">
				% Quick checks
				true  &amp; true	% → true:  1 (logical)
				true  &amp; false	% → false: 0 (logical)
				false &amp; true	% → false: 0 (logical)
				false &amp; false	% → false: 0 (logical)

				true  | true	% → true:  1 (logical)
				true  | false	% → true:  1 (logical)
				false | true	% → true:  1 (logical)
				false | false	% → false: 0 (logical)
			</program>

			<p>
				Now try these operators in context.
			</p>

			<program language="matlab">
				<code>
					P = 1;
					Q = 10;
					R = -4;

					P &lt;= R &amp; P &gt; Q	  % → false	&amp; false: 0 (logical)
					R &lt; 0 &amp; R &lt; Q	  % → true	&amp; true:  1 (logical)
					Q == R | Q == P	  % → false	| false: 0 (logical)
					P &lt; Q | P &lt; R	  % → true	| false: 1 (logical)

					~(P &lt; Q &amp; P &lt; R)  % → NOT (true &amp; false): 1 (logical)
					P &gt; Q | P &gt; R	  % → false | true:		  1 (logical)
				</code>
			</program>

			<p>
				These examples are intentionally written in a style that reads like an English sentence.
				That habit pays off later: readable conditions produce readable <pf>if</pf>-expressions.
			</p>

		<exercise label="ckpt-combining-two-expressions-1">
			<title>Combining Logical Expressions</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Given <pf>age = 25</pf> and <pf>hasLicense = true</pf>, which expression is the 
					<em>most readable way</em> to ask "Is the person at least 18 years old AND has a driver's license?"
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement>
						<p><pf>(age &gt;= 18) &amp; hasLicense</pf></p>
					</statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>Correct! This is clear and readable, using the AND operator directly with meaningful variable names.</p>
					</feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement>
						<p><pf>(age &gt;= 18) | hasLicense</pf></p>
					</statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>Incorrect. The OR operator returns true if either condition is true, but we need 
						both conditions to be true.</p>
					</feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement>
						<p><pf>age &gt;= 18 &amp; hasLicense == true</pf></p>
					</statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>While logically correct, comparing <pf>hasLicense == true</pf> is redundant and less readable. 
						Use <pf>hasLicense</pf> directly since it's already a logical value.</p>
					</feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement>
						<p><pf>~(age &lt; 18 | ~hasLicense)</pf></p>
					</statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>While logically equivalent (using De Morgan's Law), this is much harder to read. 
						Prefer the straightforward AND version for clarity.</p>
					</feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>

		<aside>
			<title><pf>xor</pf></title>
			<p><pf>xor</pf> is another logical operator available in MATLAB that tends to be used less than the operators listed here. If you are curious about it, see MATLAB's documentation.</p>
		</aside>

		<p>
			The following table summarizes every possible outcome for each of the logical operators above.
		</p>

		<table>
			<title>Logical Operators Truth Tables</title>

			<tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor" >
				<col halign="center"/>
				<col halign="center"/>
				<col halign="center"/>
				<col halign="center"/>
				<col halign="center"/>
				<row header="yes">
					<cell colspan="2">Logical Variables</cell>
					<cell colspan="3">Logical Operator Results</cell>
				</row>
				<row header="yes">
					<cell><pf>ans1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>ans2</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>ans1 &amp; ans2</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>ans1 | ans2</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>~ans1</pf></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>0</pf></cell>
					<cell><pf>1</pf></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
		</table>

		<p>
			You can combine more than two logical expressions, and you can mix <pf>~</pf>, <pf>&amp;</pf>, and <pf>|</pf>
			in the same expression. When you do, MATLAB evaluates operators in a fixed order.
			Parentheses override everything, so you can always force the grouping you intend.
		</p>

		<p>
			In general, the logical operator precedence chain is as follows:
		</p>

		<tabular halign="center">
			<row>
				<cell>1st</cell><cell/><cell>2nd</cell><cell/><cell>3rd</cell><cell/><cell>4th</cell>
			</row>
			<row>
				<cell><pf>()</pf></cell><cell>❯</cell><cell><pf>~</pf></cell><cell>❯</cell><cell><pf>&amp;</pf></cell><cell>❯</cell><cell><pf>|</pf></cell>
			</row>
		</tabular>

		<table>
			<title>Sample Logical Operator Precedence Interpretations</title>

			<tabular halign="center">
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell>MATLAB Command</cell><cell/><cell>Interpretation</cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>~A &amp; B</pf></cell><cell>➜</cell><cell><pf>(~A) &amp; B</pf></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>~A | B</pf></cell><cell>➜</cell><cell><pf>(~A) | B</pf></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>A &amp; B | C</pf></cell><cell>➜</cell><cell><pf>(A &amp; B) | C</pf></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell><pf>A | B &amp; C</pf></cell><cell>➜</cell><cell><pf>A | (B &amp; C)</pf></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>
		</table>

		<p>
			When in doubt, use parentheses. They make your code clearer to readers and reduce errors caused by
			misinterpreting precedence.
		</p>

		<exercise label="ckpt-combining-many-expressions-1">
			<title>Operator Precedence</title>

			<statement>
				<p>
					According to MATLAB's operator precedence rules, which expression illustrates how 
					<pf>A | ~B &amp; C | D</pf> is evaluated?
				</p>
			</statement>

			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement>
						<p><pf>A | ((~B) &amp; C) | D</pf></p>
					</statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>
							Correct! 
							The NOT operator <pf>~</pf> is evaluated first, followed by the AND operator <pf>&amp;</pf>,
							and finally the OR operators <pf>|</pf> are evaluated from left to right.
						</p>
					</feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement>
						<p><pf>(A | (~B)) &amp; (C | D)</pf></p>
					</statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>
							Incorrect.
							The AND operator <pf>&amp;</pf> has higher precedence than the OR operator <pf>|</pf>, 
							so this grouping is not correct.
						</p>
					</feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement>
						<p><pf>((A | ~B) &amp; C) | D</pf></p>
					</statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>
							Incorrect.
							The AND operator <pf>&amp;</pf> is evaluated before the OR operator <pf>|</pf>,
							but the NOT operator <pf>~</pf> is evaluated first.
						</p>
					</feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement>
						<p><pf>A | ~(B &amp; C) | D</pf></p>
					</statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>
							Incorrect.
							The AND operator <pf>&amp;</pf> is evaluated before the OR operator <pf>|</pf>,
							but the NOT operator <pf>~</pf> is evaluated first.
						</p>
					</feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>

	</subsection>

	<reading-questions xml:id="programming-logic-quiz">
		<title>📖 Reading Quiz</title>

		<exercise label="concepts-logical-expressions">

			<task label="cqs-logical-expressions-data-types">
				<title>Logical Data Types</title>
				<statement correct="no">
					<p>
						In MATLAB, <pf>true</pf> and <pf>1</pf> are the same.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						Incorrect. While <pf>true</pf> and <pf>1</pf> are represented by the same numeric value, 
						they have different data types: <pf>true</pf> is of type <pf>logical</pf>, 
						while <pf>1</pf> is of type <pf>double</pf>.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-logical-expressions-relational-not-equal">
				<title>Relational Operators: Inequality</title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						Which of the following commands test that <pf>x</pf> is not equal to <pf>y</pf>?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="no">
					<choice correct="no"><statement><p><pf>~(x = y)</pf></p></statement></choice>
					<choice correct="yes"><statement><p><pf>~(x == y)</pf></p></statement></choice>
					<choice correct="no"><statement><p><pf>~x == y</pf></p></statement></choice>
					<choice correct="yes"><statement><p><pf>x ~= y</pf></p></statement></choice>
					<choice correct="no"><statement><pf>x ~== y</pf></statement></choice>
				</choices>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						In MATLAB, the tilde-equals <pf>~=</pf> operator tests for inequality. 
						Note that many other languages use <pf>!=</pf>, but MATLAB uses <pf>~=</pf>.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-logical-expressions-translation-intro">
				<title>Translating Relational Operators</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Give the logical expression that is equivalent to the question:
					</p>
					<p>
						Is <m>7</m> greater than <m>3</m>? ➜ <fillin mode="string" answer="7 &gt; 3" width="10"/>
					</p>
				</statement>
				<evaluation>
					<evaluate>
						<test correct="yes">
							<strcmp>(7\s*&gt;\s*3|3\s*&lt;\s*7)</strcmp>
							<feedback><p>Correct!</p></feedback>
						</test>
						<test>
							<strcmp>.*</strcmp>
							<feedback><p>Incorrect. Use the <pf>&gt;</pf> operator to test if one value is greater than another.</p></feedback>
						</test>
					</evaluate>
				</evaluation>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<p>
						The MATLAB command is <pf>7 &gt; 3</pf>, which evaluates to <pf>1</pf> (true).
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-logical-expressions-basic-ops-result">
				<title>Basic Logical Operators</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Select the value contained in <pf>ans</pf> after evaluating the following expression:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						<code>
							ans = (3 &gt;= 2) &amp; (3+4 == 6)
						</code>
					</program>
				</statement>
				<choices>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>ans = 1</pf> (logical)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. While <pf>3 &gt;= 2</pf> is true, check the second part: is <m>3+4</m> really equal to <m>6</m>?
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>ans = 0</pf> (logical)</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! Even though <pf>3 &gt;= 2</pf> is true, the expression <pf>3+4 == 6</pf> is false (since <m>3+4=7</m>). 
								For an AND operation to be true, both sides must be true.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>ans = 1</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. The result of a logical operation is always of type <pf>logical</pf>, not <pf>double</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								<pf>ans = 0</pf>
							</p>
						</statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Incorrect. The result of a logical operation is always of type <pf>logical</pf>, not <pf>double</pf>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement>
							<p>
								None of the above. This expression will produce an error.
							</p>
						</statement>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-logical-expressions-basic-ops-explanation">
				<title>Understanding AND</title>

				<statement correct="no">
					<p>
						An AND operation (<pf>&amp;</pf>) returns true if at least one of the conditions is true.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						Incorrect. An AND operation requires <em>both</em> conditions to be true. 
						You're thinking of OR (<pf>|</pf>), which returns true if <em>at least one</em> condition is true.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-logical-expressions-combined-1">
				<title>Is p positive?</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Give the command that is equivalent to asking the question:
					</p>
					<p>
						Is <pf>p</pf> positive? ➜ <fillin mode="string" answer="p &gt; 0" width="10"/>
					</p>
				</statement>
					<evaluation>
						<evaluate>
							<test correct="yes">
								<strcmp>(p\s*&gt;\s*0|0\s*&lt;\s*p)</strcmp>
								<feedback><p>Correct!</p></feedback>
							</test>
							<test>
								<strcmp>.*</strcmp>
								<feedback><p>Incorrect. Use the <pf>&gt;</pf> operator to test if <pf>p</pf> is greater than 0.</p></feedback>
							</test>
						</evaluate>
					</evaluation>
					<solution component="example-solution">
						<p>
							<pf>ans1 = p &gt; 0</pf> evaluates to <pf>0</pf> (false) since <m>-3</m> is not positive.
						</p>
					</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-logical-expressions-combined-2">
				<title>Is p equal to 4?</title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						Give the command that is equivalent to asking the question:
					</p>
					<p>
						Is <pf>p</pf> equal to <pf>4</pf>? ➜ <fillin mode="string" answer="p == 4" width="10"/>
					</p>
				</statement>
				<evaluation>
					<evaluate>
						<test correct="yes">
							<strcmp>(p\s*==\s*4|4\s*==\s*p)</strcmp>
							<feedback><p>Correct!</p></feedback>
						</test>
						<test>
							<strcmp>.*</strcmp>
							<feedback><p>Incorrect. Use the <pf>==</pf> operator to test equality.</p></feedback>
						</test>
					</evaluate>
				</evaluation>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<p>
						<pf>ans2 = p == 4</pf> evaluates to <pf>0</pf> (false) since <m>-3 \neq 4</m>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-logical-expressions-combined-3">
				<title>Combining with AND</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Select the command that is equivalent to asking the question:
					</p>
					<p>
						Is <pf>p</pf> a negative number greater than <pf>-4</pf>?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices>
					<choice><statement><p><pf> p &lt; 0 &amp; p &gt;= -4 </pf></p></statement></choice>
					<choice><statement><p><pf> p &lt;= 0 | p &gt; -4 </pf></p></statement></choice>
					<choice><statement><p><pf> -4 &lt; p &lt; 0 </pf></p></statement></choice>
					<choice><statement><p><pf> p &lt; 0 | p &gt; -4 </pf></p></statement></choice>
					<choice correct="yes"><statement><p><pf>p &lt; 0 &amp; p &gt; -4</pf></p></statement></choice>
				</choices>
				<feedback>
					<p>The condition must be true for both parts: <pf>p</pf> is negative (<pf>p &lt; 0</pf>) AND <pf>p</pf> is greater than <pf>-4</pf> (<pf>p &gt; -4</pf>). Use the AND operator (<pf>&amp;</pf>) to combine these two conditions.</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-logical-expressions-combined-4">
				<title>Negating with NOT</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Which logical expressions are equivalent to asking the question:
					</p>
					<p>
						Is <pf>p</pf> NOT positive?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices>
					<choice correct="yes"><statement><p><pf>~(p &gt; 0)</pf></p></statement></choice>
					<choice correct="yes"><statement><p><pf>p &lt;= 0</pf></p></statement></choice>
					<choice><statement><p><pf>~p &gt; 0</pf></p></statement></choice>
					<choice><statement><p><pf>p ~&gt; 0</pf></p></statement></choice>
					<choice><statement><p><pf>p &lt; 0</pf></p></statement></choice>
				</choices>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						The NOT operator <pf>~</pf> negates a logical value, turning true into false and false into true.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-logical-expressions-combined-5">
				<title>Combining with OR</title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						Give the command that is equivalent to asking the question:
					</p>
					<p>
						Is <pf>p</pf> not equal to 5 OR greater than 7? ➜ <fillin mode="string" answer="(p ~= 5) | (p &gt; 7)" width="15"/>
					</p>
				</statement>
				<evaluation>
					<evaluate>
						<test correct="yes">
							<strcmp>\(?\s*p\s*~=\s*5\s*\)?\s*\|\s*\(?\s*p\s*&gt;\s*7\s*\)?</strcmp>
							<feedback><p>Correct!</p></feedback>
						</test>
						<test>
							<strcmp>.*</strcmp>
							<feedback><p>Incorrect. Use <pf>~=</pf> for "not equal" and <pf>|</pf> for OR.</p></feedback>
						</test>
					</evaluate>
				</evaluation>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<p>
						<pf>ans5 = (p ~= 5) | (p &gt; 7)</pf> evaluates to <pf>1</pf> (true) since <m>-3 \neq 5</m> is true.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="cqs-logical-expressions-precedence">
				<title>Operator Precedence</title>

				<statement>
					<p>
						Select the logical expression that is equivalent to the following:
					</p>
					<program>
						~A &amp; B | C
					</program>
				</statement>

				<choices randomize="no">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>((~A) &amp; B) | C</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>~(A &amp; B | C)</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>~A &amp; (B | C)</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>~(A &amp; B) | C</pf></p></statement>
					</choice>
				</choices>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						MATLAB evaluates logical operators in this order: first <pf>~</pf> (NOT), then <pf>&amp;</pf> (AND), then <pf>|</pf> (OR).
						So <pf>~A &amp; B | C</pf> is evaluated as <pf>((~A) &amp; B) | C</pf>.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="rq-logical-values-1">
				<title>What are the two logical values?</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What are the two logical values in MATLAB?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>true</pf> and <pf>false</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Correct! These are the two logical values in MATLAB.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>yes</pf> and <pf>no</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Incorrect. MATLAB uses <pf>true</pf> and <pf>false</pf>, not <pf>yes</pf> and <pf>no</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>1</pf> and <pf>0</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>These are the numeric representations, but the logical values are <pf>true</pf> and <pf>false</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>on</pf> and <pf>off</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Incorrect. MATLAB uses <pf>true</pf> and <pf>false</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="rq-logical-values-3">
				<title>Memory usage of logical values</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						How many bytes does a <pf>logical</pf> scalar use in MATLAB?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p>1 byte</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Correct! Logical values use only 1 byte, compared to 8 bytes for a <pf>double</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>8 bytes</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>That is the size of a <pf>double</pf>, not a <pf>logical</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>4 bytes</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Incorrect. Logical scalars use 1 byte in MATLAB.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>2 bytes</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Incorrect. Logical scalars use 1 byte in MATLAB.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="rq-logical-values-4">
				<statement correct="yes">
					<p>
						Logical values in MATLAB can be stored in variables just like numbers.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						True! You can assign <pf>true</pf> or <pf>false</pf> to a variable exactly like any numeric value.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

			<task label="rq-logical-values-5">
				<title>Class of a logical value</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What is the MATLAB class of the variable <pf>x</pf> after <pf>x = false</pf>?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>logical</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Correct! Assigning <pf>false</pf> creates a variable of class <pf>logical</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>double</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Incorrect. <pf>false</pf> creates a <pf>logical</pf>, not a <pf>double</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>int</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Incorrect. MATLAB does not have a class named <pf>int</pf> for this purpose.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>boolean</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Incorrect. MATLAB calls this type <pf>logical</pf>, not <pf>boolean</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="rq-logical-values-6">
				<title>Arithmetic with logical values</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What does MATLAB print after <pf>fprintf('%g\n', true + true)</pf>?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>2</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Correct! <pf>true</pf> acts as <pf>1</pf> in arithmetic, so <pf>true + true = 2</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>true</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Incorrect. MATLAB does not print the word <pf>true</pf> with <pf>fprintf('%g', ...)</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>1</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Incorrect. Adding two <pf>true</pf> values gives <pf>2</pf>, not <pf>1</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>An error occurs.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Incorrect. Logical values can participate in arithmetic; no error is raised.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="rq-logical-values-7">
				<statement correct="no">
					<p>
						MATLAB will produce an error if you use a logical value in an arithmetic expression.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						False. MATLAB allows logical values in arithmetic, treating <pf>true</pf> as <pf>1</pf> and <pf>false</pf> as <pf>0</pf>.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-logical-statements-1">
			<title>Assignment vs. Comparison</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Which operator tests whether two values are equal?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p><pf>==</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct! Double equals tests equality.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>=</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Single equals assigns a value; it does not compare.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>~=</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>This tests for inequality (not equal), not equality.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>=&gt;</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>This is not a valid MATLAB operator. Greater-than-or-equal uses <pf>&gt;=</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-logical-statements-2">
			<statement correct="no">
				<p>
					An AND operation (<pf>&amp;</pf>) returns true if at least one of the conditions is true.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<feedback>
				<p>
					False. AND requires <em>both</em> conditions to be true. You are thinking of OR (<pf>|</pf>), which returns true if at least one is true.
				</p>
			</feedback>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-logical-statements-3">
			<title>Result of a comparison</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					What is the value of <pf>p</pf> after <pf>p = (10 &gt; 5)</pf>?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p><pf>1</pf> (logical)</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct! Since 10 is greater than 5, the expression evaluates to <pf>true</pf>, which displays as <pf>1</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>0</pf> (logical)</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Incorrect. 10 is greater than 5, so the result is true.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>1</pf> (double)</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>The numeric value is correct, but the class is <pf>logical</pf>, not <pf>double</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>An error occurs.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>No error. This is a valid comparison that produces a logical result.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-logical-statements-4">
			<title>Negation result</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					What does <pf>~(3 &gt; 5)</pf> evaluate to?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p><pf>1</pf> (true)</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct! <pf>3 &gt; 5</pf> is false, and NOT false is true.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>0</pf> (false)</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Incorrect. <pf>3 &gt; 5</pf> is false; negating it gives true.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>An error occurs.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>No error. The NOT operator negates any logical value.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>-1</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Incorrect. NOT produces <pf>true</pf> or <pf>false</pf>, not <pf>-1</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-logical-statements-5">
			<statement correct="yes">
				<p>
					The expression <pf>x &lt;= 10</pf> is equivalent to <pf>~(x &gt; 10)</pf>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<feedback>
				<p>
					True. "Not greater than 10" is the same as "less than or equal to 10."
				</p>
			</feedback>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-logical-statements-6">
			<title>AND evaluation</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Given <pf>x = 5</pf>, what does <pf>(x &gt; 0) &amp; (x &lt; 3)</pf> evaluate to?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p><pf>0</pf> (false)</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct! While <pf>5 &gt; 0</pf> is true, <pf>5 &lt; 3</pf> is false. AND requires both to be true.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>1</pf> (true)</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Incorrect. <pf>5 &lt; 3</pf> is false, so the AND expression is false.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>An error occurs.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>No error. This is a valid logical expression.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>5</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Incorrect. Logical expressions produce <pf>0</pf> or <pf>1</pf>, not the value of <pf>x</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-logical-statements-7">
			<title>OR evaluation</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Given <pf>x = 5</pf>, what does <pf>(x &lt; 0) | (x &gt; 4)</pf> evaluate to?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p><pf>1</pf> (true)</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct! Although <pf>5 &lt; 0</pf> is false, <pf>5 &gt; 4</pf> is true. OR requires only one to be true.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>0</pf> (false)</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Incorrect. <pf>5 &gt; 4</pf> is true, so the OR is true.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>An error occurs.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>No error. This is a valid logical expression.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>2</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Incorrect. Logical expressions produce <pf>0</pf> or <pf>1</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-logical-statements-8">
			<title>Precedence: NOT before AND</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					How does MATLAB interpret <pf>~A &amp; B</pf>?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p><pf>(~A) &amp; B</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct! NOT has higher precedence than AND, so <pf>A</pf> is negated first.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>~(A &amp; B)</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Incorrect. NOT applies only to <pf>A</pf>, not to the whole AND expression.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>A &amp; (~B)</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Incorrect. The NOT operator is placed before <pf>A</pf>, so it negates <pf>A</pf>, not <pf>B</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>This causes an error.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>No error. This is valid MATLAB syntax.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-logical-statements-9">
			<statement correct="no">
				<p>
					In MATLAB, <pf>A &amp; B | C</pf> is evaluated as <pf>A &amp; (B | C)</pf>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<feedback>
				<p>
					False. AND has higher precedence than OR, so it is evaluated as <pf>(A &amp; B) | C</pf>.
				</p>
			</feedback>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-logical-statements-10">
			<title>Inequality operator</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Which operator is used in MATLAB to test "not equal to"?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p><pf>~=</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct! Many other languages use <pf>!=</pf>, but MATLAB uses <pf>~=</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>!=</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Incorrect. MATLAB uses <pf>~=</pf> for "not equal to."</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>&lt;&gt;</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Incorrect. MATLAB does not use <pf>&lt;&gt;</pf>; use <pf>~=</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>=/=</pf></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Incorrect. Use <pf>~=</pf> for inequality in MATLAB.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>

	</reading-questions>

	<reading-questions xml:id="programming-logic-coding-practice">
		<title>🧑🏻‍💻 Coding Practice</title>

		<introduction>
			<p>
				These coding activities focus on writing short MATLAB functions that use <pf>if</pf> statements.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<exercise label="logical-statements-activity-trace">
			<title>Trace Logical Expressions</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					For each expression, determine the result without running the code. Then verify in the cell below.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task label="logical-statements-activity-trace-a">
				<statement>
					<program language="matlab">
						x = 8;
						result = (x &gt; 5) &amp; (x &lt; 10);
					</program>
					<p>What is the value of <pf>result</pf>?</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							% ==== Type your code here ====
							x = 8;
							result = (x &gt; 5) &amp; (x &lt; 10);
							fprintf('result = %d\n', result)
						</input>
					</sage>
					<p>When done, confirm it prints <pf>result = 1</pf>.</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<p>
						<pf>result = 1</pf> (true). Both <pf>8 &gt; 5</pf> (true) and <pf>8 &lt; 10</pf> (true) are true, so the AND result is true.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="logical-statements-activity-trace-b">
				<statement>
					<program language="matlab">
						a = 3;
						b = 7;
						result = ~(a == b) | (a &gt; b);
					</program>
					<p>What is the value of <pf>result</pf>?</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							% ==== Type your code here ====
							a = 3;
							b = 7;
							result = ~(a == b) | (a &gt; b);
							fprintf('result = %d\n', result)
						</input>
					</sage>
					<p>When done, confirm it prints <pf>result = 1</pf>.</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<p>
						<pf>result = 1</pf> (true). <pf>~(3 == 7)</pf> is <pf>~false = true</pf>; since the first part of OR is true, the whole expression is true.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

		</exercise>

		<exercise label="logical-statements-activity-write">
			<title>Write Logical Expressions from English</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					Translate each English question into a MATLAB logical expression. Use the <pf>sage</pf> cell to test your answers with sample values.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task label="logical-statements-activity-write-a">
				<statement>
					<p>
						"Is <pf>score</pf> at least 60 but less than 70?"
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							% ==== Type your code here ====
							score = 65;   % change to test different values

							% =============================
						</input>
					</sage>
					<p>When <pf>score = 65</pf>, the result should be <pf>1</pf> (true). When <pf>score = 72</pf>, it should be <pf>0</pf> (false).</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						score = 65;
						result = (score &gt;= 60) &amp; (score &lt; 70);
						fprintf('result = %d\n', result)
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="logical-statements-activity-write-b">
				<statement>
					<p>
						"Is <pf>n</pf> outside the range 0 to 100?" (i.e., less than 0 or greater than 100)
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							% ==== Type your code here ====
							n = 50;   % change to test different values

							% =============================
						</input>
					</sage>
					<p>When <pf>n = 50</pf>, the result should be <pf>0</pf> (false). When <pf>n = 110</pf>, it should be <pf>1</pf> (true).</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						n = 50;
						result = (n &lt; 0) | (n &gt; 100);
						fprintf('result = %d\n', result)
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

		</exercise>
	</reading-questions>

</section>